<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chief Pleas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1565">1565</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chief Pleas is the parliament of Sark in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/channel-islands">Channel Islands</a>. It began with the holders of the forty tenements into which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helier-de-carteret">Helier de Carteret</a> divided the island when he settled it under a charter from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> in 1565. Reforms in 2008 replaced the tenants' seats with elected members, ending what was often called the last feudal system in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chief-pleas.jpg" alt="Chief Pleas" width="320" height="364" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HaGlaSu · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sark_Chief_Pleas_Seal.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>For most of its history the Chief Pleas was not elected. It consisted of the hereditary Seigneur, the Seneschal, who was then appointed by the Seigneur, and the owners of the island's forty tenements. It met only now and then to hear the pleas of its members, and this gave the assembly its name.</p><p>In 1922 the assembly was enlarged to include 12 Deputies chosen by popular vote, for which only those who were not tenants could stand. In 1957, after residents appealed to the Lieutenant Governor of Guernsey, 12 People's Deputies were introduced.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Sark was the last jurisdiction to abolish its feudal regime. In 2006 the Chief Pleas voted by 25 to 15 to reform itself, cutting its membership from 52 to 28, with 14 landowners and 14 residents to be elected. The Privy Council endorsed the law in 2008, and Sark held its first democratic election to the Chief Pleas on 10 December 2008.</p><p>A further reform, decided in 2010 and in force from 2013, split the office of Seneschal, who until then had been both president of the Chief Pleas and the island's chief judge, after concerns that the double role did not comply with human rights law. From 2013 the assembly had presidents of its own, and from 2017 a Speaker.</p><p>The Chief Pleas is a non-partisan assembly of 20 members: 18 directly elected Conseillers, the Speaker, whom the Conseillers elect, and the Seigneur, the last two without a vote. Conseillers serve four years, and half the seats are contested every two years. The island's administration is run through committees of Conseillers, some with members from outside the assembly, covering such matters as agriculture and sea fisheries, harbours and shipping, education, medical and emergency services, and policy and finance.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chief Pleas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Remnants lasted far longer: the last seigneurial rents in Quebec were paid in 1970, Scotland ended feudal land tenure only in 2004, and Sark replaced the tenants’ seats in its parliament, the <b>Chief Pleas</b>, with elected members in 2008.</blockquote><a href="/government/feudalism">Feudalism · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>He divided it into forty tenements, whose holders formed its parliament, the <b>Chief Pleas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/sark">Sark</a></li></ul></section>
